## Starting From Zero

Building a design system isn't about creating beautiful components. It's about creating a **language** that your entire team speaks fluently.

### Lesson 1: Tokens Before Components

The biggest mistake teams make is jumping straight to building a Button component. Start with design tokens instead.

```css
:root {
  /* Spacing scale */
  --space-1: 4px;
  --space-2: 8px;
  --space-3: 12px;
  --space-4: 16px;
  --space-6: 24px;
  --space-8: 32px;

  /* Typography scale */
  --text-xs: 0.75rem;
  --text-sm: 0.875rem;
  --text-base: 1rem;
  --text-lg: 1.125rem;

  /* Colors - semantic, not literal */
  --color-primary: #3553ff;
  --color-surface: #fafaf5;
  --color-text: #1a1a1a;
  --color-text-muted: #4a4a4a;
}
```

### Lesson 2: Composition Over Configuration

Don't build a Button with 47 props. Build small, composable pieces.

```tsx
// ❌ The mega-component approach
<Button
  variant="primary"
  size="large"
  icon={<Icon />}
  iconPosition="left"
  loading={true}
  loadingText="Saving..."
  fullWidth={true}
/>

// ✅ The composition approach
<Button variant="primary" size="lg">
  <Button.Icon><SaveIcon /></Button.Icon>
  <Button.Label>Save changes</Button.Label>
  <Button.Spinner />
</Button>
```

### Lesson 3: Document Everything (Seriously)

Every component needs:
- **Usage guidelines** — When to use it, when NOT to use it
- **Props table** — Types, defaults, descriptions
- **Examples** — Real-world usage, not contrived demos
- **Accessibility notes** — ARIA roles, keyboard navigation

### Lesson 4: Say No More Than Yes

The hardest part of maintaining a design system is **not** adding things. Every component you add is a component you maintain forever.

My rule of thumb: If fewer than 3 teams need it, it's not a design system component. It's a product component.

### The Architecture That Worked

```
design-system/
├── tokens/           # Design tokens (JSON → CSS/TS)
├── primitives/       # Unstyled, accessible base components
├── components/       # Themed, styled components
├── patterns/         # Common compositions (forms, cards, etc.)
└── docs/             # Storybook + usage guides
```

### Key Metrics We Tracked

- **Adoption rate** — % of product UI using system components
- **Contribution rate** — PRs from outside the core team
- **Bug density** — Bugs per component per quarter
- **Time to build** — How fast teams ship new features

After 18 months, our adoption rate hit 87% and average feature delivery time dropped by 40%. That's the real measure of a design system's success.
